{
  "$schema": "https://docs.oasis-open.org/sarif/sarif/v2.1.0/cos02/schemas/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Graphite Compliance Auditor",
          "organization": "Graphite AI Safety",
          "semanticVersion": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/graphite-ai/graphite",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "EU_AI_ACT:Art.5",
              "name": "EU_AI_ACT_Art.5",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Untargeted internet/CCTV acquisition builds a facial-recognition database"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "CONFIRMED_VIOLATION: 4 source files configure untargeted bulk acquisition (public-web and CCTV discovery, public accessibility treated as sufficient for admission, recurring expansion), and 8 files implement facial/biometric identification or indexing. Together, these elements (admission based solely on public accessibility, ingestion that does not filter to enrolled subjects, features added to the searchable index, and recurring expansion) model the Art. 5(1)(e) prohibition on creating or expanding facial-recognition databases through untargeted scraping of facial images from the internet or CCTV. Safeguards such as logging, accuracy measures or human approval of searches do not cure the potential inconsistency. This is a modeled practice: confirm actual acquisition methods and deployment facts before making a real-world legal finding. Basis: EU AI Act Art. 5. Related: EU_GDPR:Art.44. (Technical audit observation, not legal advice; subject to human review.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "CONFIRMED_VIOLATION"
                ],
                "precision": "very-high"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_AI_ACT:Art.5\nSeverity: CRITICAL\nExposure: Potential exposure: administrative fines of up to EUR 35m or 7% of worldwide annual turnover for prohibited practices (EU AI Act Art. 99), plus possible market withdrawal.\n\nRemediation Steps:\n- Remove the untargeted acquisition workflow: disable public_web_bulk / cctv_bulk source admission and the recurring expansion jobs that populate the facial index.\n- Purge facial features collected through untargeted discovery from the identity index, and keep only subjects who directly enrolled.\n- Restrict admission to specifically enrolled subjects (scope=enrolled_subjects, acquisitionMethod=subject_submission) and enforce it in code, not only in policy.\n- Do not rely on audit logs, human approval of each search or better accuracy: they do not cure an Art. 5(1)(e) acquisition practice. Reassess the replacement product separately against the remaining AI Act requirements."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EU_GDPR:Art.35",
              "name": "EU_GDPR_Art.35",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No DPIA evidenced for high-risk processing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Suspected gap: The system processes personal data or makes automated decisions, yet no document references a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) as required by GDPR Art. 35 for processing likely to result in high risk to individuals, including systematic and extensive automated processing, particularly with new technologies. (Technical audit observation, not legal advice; subject to human review.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "SUSPECTED_GAP"
                ],
                "precision": "medium"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_GDPR:Art.35\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nExposure: Potential exposure: administrative fines of up to EUR 20m or 4% of worldwide annual turnover for breaches of data-subject rights, special-category and transfer rules (GDPR Art. 83(5)); up to EUR 10m or 2% for controller/processor obligations such as Arts. 28, 32 and 35 (Art. 83(4)).\n\nRemediation Steps:\n- Complete and approve a DPIA before the processing starts; review it when the system changes."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EU_AI_ACT:Art.11",
              "name": "EU_AI_ACT_Art.11",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing documentary evidence of Annex IV technical documentation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Suspected gap: no document section addresses Annex IV technical documentation. Under EU AI Act Art. 11, providers of high-risk AI systems must draw up technical documentation before placing the system on the market, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV. Related: EU_AI_ACT:AnnexIII. (Technical audit observation, not legal advice; subject to human review.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "SUSPECTED_GAP"
                ],
                "precision": "medium"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_AI_ACT:Art.11\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nExposure: Potential exposure: fines of up to EUR 15m or 3% of worldwide annual turnover (EU AI Act Art. 99) plus regulator enforcement action.\n\nRemediation Steps:\n- Compile Annex IV technical documentation and keep it versioned."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EU_GDPR:Art.22",
              "name": "EU_GDPR_Art.22",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Automated-decision handling could not be located"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Status: SUSPECTED_GAP. No decision entry point was identified, so the Art. 22 safeguards (human intervention and a way to contest solely automated decisions with significant effects) are unproven. Basis: GDPR Art. 22, which gives data subjects the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, that produces legal or similar effects. (Technical audit observation, not legal advice; subject to human review.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "SUSPECTED_GAP"
                ],
                "precision": "medium"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_GDPR:Art.22\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nExposure: Potential exposure: administrative fines of up to EUR 20m or 4% of worldwide annual turnover for breaches of data-subject rights, special-category and transfer rules (GDPR Art. 83(5)); up to EUR 10m or 2% for controller/processor obligations such as Arts. 28, 32 and 35 (Art. 83(4)).\n\nRemediation Steps:\n- Add a human review step before consequential automated decisions take effect, and a documented route for people to obtain human intervention and contest the decision."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EU_AI_ACT:Art.14",
              "name": "EU_AI_ACT_Art.14",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Human-oversight gate could not be located"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Suspected gap: No decision entry point was identified, so the presence of a human approval gate before consequential automated decisions take effect is unproven. Basis: EU AI Act Art. 14, which requires high-risk AI systems to be designed so natural persons can effectively oversee them, including the ability to intervene, override or stop the system. Related: EU GDPR Art. 22. (Technical audit observation, not legal advice; subject to human review.)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "SUSPECTED_GAP"
                ],
                "precision": "medium"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_AI_ACT:Art.14\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nExposure: Potential exposure: fines of up to EUR 15m or 3% of worldwide annual turnover (EU AI Act Art. 99) plus regulator enforcement action.\n\nRemediation Steps:\n- Route consequential decisions through a human approval queue before release.\n- Log reviewer identity, decision and override reason (Art. 12 record-keeping)."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "EU_AI_ACT:Art.10",
              "name": "EU_AI_ACT_Art.10",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PRIVACY promise consistent with code"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "PASS: The implementation matches the documented PII-masking promise, as redaction is present (or there are no external flows). The policy claim (PRIVACY) states that the adapter serializes no credentials or face data, only a validated versioned tenant/job reference. Basis: EU AI Act Art. 10, which requires appropriate data governance for training, validation and testing data sets, including measures for bias, data minimisation and data protection. Related: UK_GDPR_ICO:Art.22."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "1.0",
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "regulatory",
                  "QUARANTINED_FOR_HUMAN_REVIEW"
                ],
                "precision": "medium"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Statutory Mandate: EU_AI_ACT:Art.10\nSeverity: INFO\nExposure: \n\nRemediation Steps:\n"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
